.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd October 17, 2026
.Dt NET-STATUS 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm net-status
.Nd inspect the SSH owner's retained static network sample
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
shows the static IPv4 address, prefix, gateway, SSH port and input origin
actually consumed by the SSH owner, its device carrier and disabled reading,
and local listener state.
Draining means an operator shutdown or reboot is latched and new sessions
are no longer accepted; it does not establish that unlisten succeeded.
It is published only if a fresh device read follows before the owner exits;
a quick exit may leave the earlier retained listening sample.
The fixed sample is in
.Pa /var/log/network-status ;
sessions can read it but cannot write it.
.Pp
The output names the boot and served recovery sequence, the device read's
kernel tick interval and the retained sample's age.
Stable state is published at most once per 3600000 raw ticks
(approximately hourly on the pinned x86 PIT), with earlier device or local
listener-phase transitions.
A sample older than 3615000 raw ticks is stale; its age may therefore be
about an hour even while the owner keeps reading the device.
The heartbeat is retained evidence, not a service-liveness check.
An old boot, superseded recovery sequence, unavailable identity, malformed or
missing sample, future interval or changed recovery during acquisition is
reported as unavailable instead of a successful network observation.
An unknown device read never repeats an older up indication.
.Pp
A retained sample is not proof that its service is alive now, that a remote
peer is reachable or that every network event was retained.
The command neither probes a peer nor changes the network.
It does not report DHCP, IPv6 or DAD probe state.
The existing file server call can stall; a caller needs its own acquisition
timeout.
.Pp
The owner overwrites a fixed 512-byte file on transitions and periodically.
It does not append periodic records to the SSH log or console.
Every publication can change mtime and cause a full filesystem metadata
commit. Stable state causes at most about 24 heartbeat publications per
nominal day after readiness; transitions add writes. This is not a bound on
physical media writes or commits caused by other filesystem activity.
The current recovery binding comes from the file server's fixed 128-byte
.Pa /var/log/network-recovery
projection, checked against the current kernel boot before and after
reading the network sample.
It uses the existing recovery-ledger encoding; the canonical ledger stays
private.
The file server writes it, and sshd's existing control mount can also write it.
It is not an independent file-server attestation.
Future ledger versions must update this public diagnostic consumer together.
The file server attempts publication only at startup or recovery, before readiness.
It repairs wrong-shaped objects at this diagnostic path.
Failure logs a named unavailable record and still permits readiness, so an
operator can log in to free space; failed copies are best-effort removed.
Log rotation does not remove it.
A missing or invalid projection makes the binding unavailable, without a
fallback to log history.
This does not establish a latest live server identity.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Status 1 means that the sample or binding is unavailable, or that the device
reading is unknown.
Status 2 is usage error.
A successful sample does not establish reachability or general system health.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr health 1 ,
.Xr network.conf 5 ,
.Xr afterboot 8
